@startuml 09_components
!include ../style.iuml
title Component diagram (overview): ClaimGuard AI

actor "Reviewer or\nAdministrator" as H

component "Reviewer API\n(FastAPI)" as API
component "Access service\n(sign-in, tokens, rights,\nidentifier masking)" as ACC
component "Work queue\n(intake, triage, dealing,\ndecisions, sign-off)" as QUEUE
component "Checking engine\n(15 YARA-X rules +\nadvisory checks)" as ENG
component "Model adapter\n(validation and guard\nof the replies)" as ADA
component "Audit log\n(hash chain, anchor)" as AUD
component "Security log\n(signed hash chain)" as SEC

database "MongoDB\n(dossiers, decisions, deals,\nusers, revoked tokens)" as MDB
database "Redis\n(message broker)" as RDS
cloud "Language model\n(local Ollama or hosted)" as MOD

H --> API : HTTPS\n(cookie + CSRF)
API ..> ACC : authenticate,\npermissions
API ..> QUEUE : inbox, decisions,\ndashboard, feedback
ACC ..> SEC
ACC ..> MDB
QUEUE ..> ENG : evaluate
QUEUE ..> AUD : review decisions
QUEUE ..> SEC
QUEUE ..> MDB
QUEUE ..> RDS : messages
QUEUE ..> ADA : injected model
ADA ..> MOD

note right of QUEUE
  Claims are submitted with the command-line tool
  (queue_admin), not over HTTP. The production
  model is not wired in yet: without a model the
  deterministic text is kept.
end note

note bottom of ENG
  The 15 rules give the official verdict. The advisory
  checks are advice only, never mixed into the verdict.
end note
@enduml
